  0 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: Have you seen the notice from the academic board about switching some of our final exams to an open-book format next semester? They\'re considering it for the more theory-heavy modules, where students would be allowed to bring textbooks and notes into the exam hall. They want feedback from student representatives before finalizing the policy, so I thought we should talk it through.
Speaker 2: I\'m honestly excited about this idea. Traditional closed-book exams put so much pressure on memorizing facts and formulas rather than actually understanding the material. With an open-book format, you\'re tested on how well you can apply concepts and analyze problems, which feels like a far more realistic measure of learning, and it removes some of that panic when your mind goes blank under pressure.
Speaker 3: I actually disagree, at least partly. From what I\'ve heard, open-book exams are often harder, not easier, because the questions get designed to require deeper analysis rather than simple recall. If your notes aren\'t well organized, you can waste half the exam flipping through pages looking for the right formula, which could disadvantage students who don\'t have tidy study habits.
Speaker 1: That\'s a fair concern. Maybe instead of full open-book access, we could suggest a compromise where students are allowed one page of handwritten notes, like a cheat sheet, rather than entire textbooks. That way there\'s still an incentive to review and understand the material well enough to summarize it, but some of the pure memorization pressure is removed.
Speaker 2: I really like that compromise. Preparing a single summary sheet forces you to engage with the material and decide what\'s actually important, which is a useful skill in itself, without eliminating the need to study.
Speaker 3: I could get behind that, especially if it\'s limited to the more theoretical modules rather than every course. I\'ll suggest we recommend the one-page cheat sheet approach for theory-based subjects as our group\'s feedback to the board.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group was discussing a proposal from the academic board to switch some final exams to an open-book format, especially for theory-heavy modules. The first speaker just introduced the topic and asked for opinions. One person was strongly in favour, arguing that closed-book exams focus too much on memorization rather than understanding, and that open-book testing measures real application of knowledge while reducing panic under pressure. The next person disagreed, pointing out that open-book exams are often actually harder because questions are designed to require deeper analysis, and that students with disorganized notes could waste time searching for information, putting them at a disadvantage. The first speaker then proposed a compromise: instead of full access to textbooks, students could bring a single page of handwritten notes, which would still require them to understand and summarize the material without full memorization pressure. The second speaker liked this idea, noting that creating a summary sheet is itself a valuable learning exercise. The third speaker agreed too, suggesting it should apply mainly to theoretical courses, and the group settled on recommending the one-page cheat sheet approach to the board.',
