  1 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: Did you hear about the new attendance policy the department is proposing? They want to make eighty percent lecture attendance mandatory, and if you fall below that, you wouldn\'t be allowed to sit the final exam at all. They\'re asking student reps to weigh in before it goes to the faculty vote.
Speaker 2: I actually think that\'s a reasonable move. So many people skip lectures and then struggle when exam season comes around, because they never built the habit of engaging regularly with the material. Attendance tends to correlate pretty strongly with performance, and a policy like this might just push people to show up and stay on track.
Speaker 3: I see where you\'re coming from, but I think it\'s a bit heavy-handed. Some students genuinely learn better by working independently from recorded materials or textbooks, and not every lecture is equally useful. Blocking someone from an exam because of attendance numbers, rather than their actual knowledge, seems unfair and honestly a little patronizing for adults who are paying for their own education.
Speaker 1: That\'s true. Maybe instead of tying attendance directly to exam eligibility, we could suggest it counts toward a small participation grade instead, say five or ten percent of the total mark. That way it still encourages people to show up without completely barring anyone from the final assessment.
Speaker 2: That actually sounds like a decent middle ground. It keeps the incentive to attend without punishing someone so severely for a few missed sessions, and it still rewards the students who do consistently turn up.
Speaker 3: Agreed, I think that\'s much fairer. I\'ll note down that our group supports linking attendance to a small participation component rather than making it a strict requirement for exam entry.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group was talking about a proposed new attendance policy requiring students to attend at least eighty percent of lectures, with those falling below that threshold being barred from sitting the final exam. The first speaker introduced the proposal and asked for reactions. One person supported it, arguing that attendance correlates with performance and that mandatory attendance would help students build good habits instead of falling behind. The next speaker disagreed, saying the rule felt heavy-handed, since some students learn better independently and it seemed unfair to block someone from an exam based on attendance numbers rather than actual knowledge, calling it patronizing. The first speaker then suggested a middle ground: instead of barring students from exams, attendance could count toward a small participation grade, maybe five to ten percent of the total mark. The second speaker agreed this was a good compromise, since it still encourages attendance without being overly punitive. The third speaker also came around to this idea, and the group concluded they would recommend linking attendance to a minor participation grade rather than making it a strict exam requirement.',
    'voices' => [
