  2 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: So the IT committee is proposing a ban on laptops in lecture halls, apparently because of complaints about students getting distracted by browsing or messaging during class. They want to know what students think before rolling it out across all departments next term.
Speaker 2: I\'m actually in favor of this. There\'s a lot of research showing that laptop use during lectures lowers retention, even for the person using it, and it distracts the people sitting nearby who can see the screen. Going back to handwritten notes might slow people down a bit, but that slower pace actually helps with remembering things better.
Speaker 3: I understand that concern, but a full ban seems impractical to me. Plenty of students rely on laptops for accessibility reasons, like screen readers or speech-to-text software, and in technical courses like computer science, you genuinely need a laptop to follow along with live coding examples. A blanket ban would end up punishing people who use their laptops responsibly.
Speaker 1: That\'s a good point. Maybe the compromise is to make it course-specific rather than a university-wide rule. Technical or lab-based classes could keep laptops allowed, while large lecture-style courses in humanities or social sciences, where laptops are more likely to be used for note-taking rather than active coding, could designate laptop-free sections or rows.
Speaker 2: I think that works well. It targets the courses where distraction is most likely to be an issue, without cutting off students who genuinely need their laptops for the subject or for accessibility reasons.
Speaker 3: Agreed, that seems like the fairest approach. I\'ll suggest we recommend leaving the decision to individual lecturers, with designated laptop-free seating as an option rather than a blanket ban.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group was discussing a proposal to ban laptops in lecture halls due to concerns about distraction. The first speaker introduced the idea, explaining that the IT committee wanted student feedback before rolling it out. One person supported the ban, citing research that laptop use lowers retention and distracts nearby students, arguing that handwriting notes helps with memory even if it\'s slower. The next speaker disagreed, pointing out that some students need laptops for accessibility tools like screen readers, and that technical courses such as computer science require laptops for live coding, so a blanket ban would unfairly punish responsible users. The first speaker then proposed a compromise: rather than a university-wide ban, individual lecturers could decide, with technical courses keeping laptops allowed and large lecture-style humanities courses offering designated laptop-free sections. The second speaker agreed this targeted the real problem areas without excluding students who need laptops. The third speaker also agreed, and the group concluded that they would recommend leaving the decision to lecturers, with optional laptop-free seating rather than an outright ban.',
    'voices' => [
      'af_nicole',
      'af_aoede',
      'am_echo',
    ],
  ],
  3 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: The programme director wants to increase the weight of group projects to forty percent of our final grade next year, cutting down on individual essays and exams. She\'s asked for our thoughts before the change is confirmed for the new curriculum.
Speaker 2: I think that\'s a great direction. Employers constantly say they want graduates with teamwork experience, and group projects mimic real workplace situations far better than a solitary essay ever could. Learning to divide tasks, negotiate ideas, and manage conflicts within a team is a skill in itself, and it deserves real weight in our grades.
Speaker 3: I hear that, but forty percent feels like a lot, especially given how group work usually goes. There\'s almost always someone who does the bare minimum while others end up carrying the workload, and it\'s incredibly hard for lecturers to fairly assess individual contribution. Getting a poor grade because of a lazy teammate feels deeply unjust when so much of your final mark depends on it.
Speaker 1: That\'s a legitimate worry. Perhaps we could suggest keeping the group weighting moderate, closer to twenty-five percent, but pairing it with a compulsory peer evaluation component that adjusts each person\'s individual mark based on their actual contribution to the project.
Speaker 2: I\'m fine with that adjustment. It keeps the collaborative element strong while still protecting people from being dragged down by someone else\'s lack of effort, which honestly makes the whole system feel more trustworthy.
Speaker 3: That works for me too. I\'ll write down that our group supports a moderate group-work weighting combined with mandatory peer evaluation to keep grading fair for everyone involved.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group was talking about a proposal to increase group projects to forty percent of the final grade, replacing some individual essays and exams. The first speaker introduced the plan and asked for opinions. One person was strongly supportive, arguing that group work builds real teamwork skills valued by employers and mirrors workplace collaboration better than solitary assignments. The next speaker pushed back, saying forty percent was too high given the common free-rider problem, where some students do minimal work while others carry the team, making fair grading nearly impossible and potentially unjust for hardworking students. The first speaker then suggested a compromise: lowering the group weighting to around twenty-five percent while adding a mandatory peer evaluation component that would adjust individual grades based on actual contribution. The second speaker accepted this, feeling it preserved the collaborative benefits while protecting students from unfair outcomes. The third speaker also agreed, and the group concluded that they would recommend a moderate group-project weighting paired with compulsory peer evaluation as their official feedback.',
    'voices' => [
      'am_michael',
      'bf_isabella',
      'af_aoede',
    ],
  ],
  4 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: The student council put forward a proposal to keep the main library open twenty-four hours a day throughout the whole term, not just during exam season like it currently is. They want our group\'s input before presenting it to the university administration.
Speaker 2: I would love that honestly. During exam periods the library gets packed and it\'s nearly impossible to find a seat after dinner time. Having it open all the time would mean people could study whenever suits their schedule, especially those juggling part-time jobs or just working better late at night.
Speaker 3: I get the appeal, but I think the cost involved would be significant. You\'d need security staff and cleaning crews working all night year-round, not just for a few exam weeks, and outside of exam season the actual demand for a fully staffed twenty-four-hour library is probably quite low. It seems like a lot of money for something that would be underused most of the year.
Speaker 1: That\'s a fair point about cost. Maybe we should propose keeping the twenty-four-hour access only during exam periods, as it is now, but extending the regular opening hours during the rest of the term, say until two in the morning instead of the current midnight closing.
Speaker 2: I could accept that. It\'s not quite what I originally wanted, but extending hours to two a.m. during normal term time would still help a lot of people who study late, and it avoids wasting money on an empty building at four in the morning in the middle of a quiet week.
Speaker 3: Agreed, that seems far more sensible financially. I\'ll note that our recommendation is to keep full twenty-four-hour access limited to exam periods, with extended but not round-the-clock hours the rest of the term.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group discussed a student council proposal to keep the main library open twenty-four hours a day for the entire term, not just during exams. The first speaker introduced the idea for the group\'s feedback. One person was very supportive, pointing out that the library gets overcrowded during exam season and that round-the-clock access would help students with different schedules, including those with part-time jobs or who prefer studying late at night. The next speaker raised concerns about cost, noting that staffing security and cleaning around the clock all year would be expensive, especially since demand outside exam season is probably much lower, making it wasteful. The first speaker then proposed a middle ground: keep full twenty-four-hour access limited to exam periods as it already is, but extend regular hours during the rest of term, for example until two in the morning instead of midnight. The second speaker accepted this, agreeing it addressed most late-night study needs without wasting money on an empty building. The third speaker agreed as well, and the group settled on recommending extended, but not fully round-the-clock, hours outside of exam season.',
