  5 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: I saw that the housing office is proposing to make it mandatory for all first-year students to live in campus dormitories next year, rather than letting people choose private accommodation from the start. They want student opinions before deciding whether to push it through.
Speaker 2: I actually think that\'s a good idea. Living on campus in first year really helps with social integration, especially for people moving away from home for the first time. It\'s also generally safer, with security staff and controlled access, and it\'s easier to make friends when everyone is going through the same adjustment period together in the same building.
Speaker 3: I understand the social benefits, but campus dorms are often far more expensive than shared private housing off campus. Making it mandatory would hit students from lower-income backgrounds the hardest, especially those who already have family nearby and could commute cheaply instead of paying for accommodation they don\'t strictly need.
Speaker 1: That\'s true, cost really does vary a lot between people\'s situations. Maybe the proposal could include an exemption process, so it\'s mandatory by default, but students can apply to opt out if they can demonstrate financial hardship or have family within a reasonable commuting distance.
Speaker 2: I think that works well. It keeps the social benefits for most incoming students, since campus living would still be the default expectation, while making sure it doesn\'t create unnecessary financial strain for students who genuinely have a cheaper, viable alternative.
Speaker 3: I\'m satisfied with that compromise too. I\'ll suggest we recommend mandatory on-campus housing for first years by default, with a clear exemption process for financial hardship or nearby family homes.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group was discussing a proposal to require all first-year students to live in campus dormitories rather than choosing private accommodation. The first speaker introduced the topic for the group to weigh in on. One person supported the idea, arguing that living on campus helps with social integration, is generally safer, and makes it easier for new students to bond during the adjustment period. The next speaker raised a concern about cost, noting that campus dorms are often more expensive than off-campus options, which could disadvantage lower-income students, especially those who could otherwise commute from a family home nearby. The first speaker then suggested a compromise: make campus housing mandatory by default, but allow students to apply for an exemption if they could show financial hardship or lived close enough to commute. The second speaker agreed this preserved the social benefits for most students while protecting those with a genuine cheaper alternative. The third speaker also accepted this solution, and the group concluded by recommending mandatory on-campus housing with a clear exemption process for financial hardship or nearby family homes.',
    'voices' => [
      'bm_lewis',
      'af_sky',
      'am_liam',
    ],
