  7 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: Apparently the university is considering recording every lecture and posting them online for students to watch anytime, instead of the current policy where only a handful of courses get recorded. They\'re asking student groups for input before making it standard practice everywhere.
Speaker 2: I think this would be incredibly helpful. If you\'re sick, have a family emergency, or just need to review a confusing section again, having the recording available makes a huge difference. It also helps students who process information more slowly and need to pause or rewind to properly absorb the material.
Speaker 3: I get the convenience factor, but I worry it would hurt actual attendance and participation. If everything is recorded and available later, a lot of students might just stop showing up altogether, and lectures with class discussion or debate really suffer when half the room isn\'t there to engage in real time.
Speaker 1: That\'s a valid concern, especially for discussion-based classes. Maybe we could suggest recording only the large, traditional lecture-style courses, where the format is mostly one-directional anyway, while leaving smaller seminars and discussion-based classes unrecorded to protect in-person participation and spontaneous debate.
Speaker 2: That seems like a sensible way to get the benefits without discouraging attendance everywhere. Big lecture halls rarely have much interactive discussion anyway, so recording those wouldn\'t really change the classroom dynamic much.
