Speaker 2: I think that\'s a smart way to balance both goals. It still creates a financial incentive for people who have a real alternative to driving, while making sure students who genuinely depend on their car aren\'t unfairly penalized.
Speaker 3: I can support that version of the plan. I\'ll write down that our group recommends tiered parking charges with subsidized permits available for students without viable public transport options.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group was discussing a proposal to introduce paid parking on campus, which is currently free, with the revenue going toward campus improvements. The first speaker introduced the plan and asked the others for their opinions. One person supported it, arguing that charging for parking would fund better shuttle buses and bike infrastructure while discouraging unnecessary short-distance driving. The next speaker disagreed, pointing out that many commuter students rely on cars out of necessity because public transport in their area is unreliable or doesn\'t run late enough, so charging them would add an unfair financial burden. The first speaker then proposed a tiered pricing system, where students living close to campus would pay more, while those with poor public transport access could apply for a subsidized or discounted permit. The second speaker liked this solution, saying it kept the incentive for people with real alternatives while protecting those who depend on their cars. The third speaker agreed as well, and the group concluded by recommending tiered parking fees with subsidized permits for students without viable transport options.',
