  10 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: There\'s a new proposal from the teaching and learning office to introduce mandatory peer review, where students would have to review and give feedback on a classmate\'s draft assignment before submitting their own final version. They want to know what students think before piloting it in a few courses.
Speaker 2: I actually think this could be really valuable. Reading someone else\'s approach to the same assignment often reveals things you hadn\'t considered in your own work, and giving feedback forces you to think critically about what makes an assignment strong or weak, which then improves your own writing when you go back to revise.
Speaker 3: I\'m skeptical though. Most students aren\'t trained to evaluate academic work properly, so the feedback they give could easily be unhelpful or even misleading if the reviewer misunderstands the assignment themselves. It also adds another task to an already busy schedule, and some students might not take the review seriously and just rush through it.
Speaker 1: That\'s a reasonable worry. Maybe instead of making it a compulsory graded step, we could suggest it be optional and framed purely as a practice exercise, with tutors doing a quick spot-check on a sample of the peer reviews to make sure the feedback quality stays reasonably high.
Speaker 2: I\'m fine with making it optional rather than mandatory, since students who genuinely want the extra perspective can opt in, and the tutor spot-checks would help maintain some level of quality control without adding pressure on everyone.
Speaker 3: That sounds much more manageable to me. I\'ll note that our group recommends offering peer review as an optional, ungraded practice option, with occasional tutor oversight to keep the feedback useful.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group was talking about a proposal to introduce mandatory peer review, where students would review a classmate\'s draft before submitting their own final assignment. The first speaker introduced the idea for discussion. One person was supportive, arguing that reviewing someone else\'s work reveals new perspectives and that giving feedback sharpens critical thinking, ultimately improving your own writing. The next speaker was skeptical, pointing out that students aren\'t trained evaluators, so feedback quality could be poor or misleading, and that adding another mandatory task could be rushed or taken unseriously. The first speaker then proposed a compromise: make peer review optional and framed as practice rather than a graded requirement, with tutors spot-checking a sample of reviews to maintain quality. The second speaker agreed, saying this let interested students benefit without burdening everyone, while tutor oversight would keep standards reasonable. The third speaker also accepted this version, and the group concluded by recommending peer review as an optional, ungraded practice exercise with occasional tutor oversight.',
    'voices' => [
