      'af_bella',
      'am_liam',
    ],
  ],
  39 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: Facilities announced we\'re switching to hot-desking next month, meaning nobody has an assigned desk anymore and people just sit wherever is free each day. I wanted to hear your reactions before we submit feedback on the plan.
Speaker 2: I actually think this could work well for us. It saves a lot of office space and cost since we don\'t need a permanent desk for every employee, especially with some people working hybrid schedules, and it might encourage people from different teams to mix and get to know each other.
Speaker 3: I\'m much less enthusiastic. Not having a personal desk means no photos, no plants, none of the small things that make a workspace feel like yours, and honestly it\'s a hassle to reset your monitor and chair every single morning. It also makes it harder for a team to stay closely connected if everyone\'s scattered in different spots each day.
Speaker 1: Both points make sense. What if we kept the cost-saving flexible seating idea but organized it into team neighborhoods, so each team has a general zone of desks reserved for them, while individuals still book a specific desk within that zone rather than sitting anywhere in the whole building?
Speaker 2: That still saves space overall since we wouldn\'t assign one fixed desk per person, but it makes the booking process much simpler since you\'d know roughly where to look each morning.
Speaker 3: That also solves my main issue about team cohesion, since colleagues would still cluster together in the same zone even without a permanently assigned individual desk. I think we should recommend hot-desking organized into team neighborhoods rather than fully open seating across the whole office.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group discussed a plan to switch to hot-desking, where no one has an assigned desk and employees sit wherever is available each day. The first speaker introduced the idea before submitting feedback to facilities. One person was positive, pointing out it would save office space and cost, especially with hybrid schedules, and could encourage mixing between different teams. The other person was less enthusiastic, missing the personalization of a permanent desk and finding it a hassle to reset equipment every morning, and worried it would make team cohesion harder if everyone sat in different spots daily. The first speaker then proposed organizing the flexible seating into team neighborhoods, where each team has a general reserved zone but individuals still book a specific desk within it rather than sitting anywhere in the building. The second speaker agreed this preserved the cost savings while making the booking process simpler. The third speaker also agreed, since teams would still cluster together, solving the cohesion concern. In the end, the group agreed to recommend hot-desking organized by team neighborhoods rather than fully open seating across the office.',
