  40 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: Did you see that the government is considering new rules requiring social media influencers to clearly label sponsored posts? Apparently a lot of people don\'t realize when a product review is actually a paid advertisement, and regulators think this is misleading consumers, especially younger audiences who trust these personalities.
Speaker 2: I think that\'s long overdue, honestly. I\'ve followed fitness accounts that recommend supplements without ever mentioning they\'re being paid by the company. It feels dishonest, and it can even be dangerous if people buy products based on fake enthusiasm rather than genuine experience. Clear labels would restore some trust.
Speaker 3: I get the concern, but I worry this could be really hard to enforce and might just push everything into a grey area. Influencers could use vague hashtags that technically comply but don\'t actually inform anyone. Plus, some creators genuinely like products before ever being approached by a brand, so labelling every mention feels excessive.
Speaker 1: That\'s fair, maybe the rule shouldn\'t apply to every single mention, but specifically to content created as part of a paid partnership or when free products are received. If platforms built a mandatory disclosure tool into the app itself, like a toggle that automatically tags the post, it would remove the ambiguity you\'re describing.
Speaker 2: I like that solution because it takes the responsibility off individual wording choices and makes it a platform-level requirement, so there\'s no room for a vague hashtag loophole. It also protects honest influencers from being lumped in with the dishonest ones.
Speaker 3: Agreed, if the platform itself handles the disclosure automatically whenever compensation or free products are involved, that solves my enforcement worry too. I think we can recommend that approach: mandatory platform-level tagging for paid content rather than relying on influencers to self-police.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group discussed a proposed rule requiring social media influencers to clearly disclose when their posts are paid advertisements, since many followers don\'t realize a review is sponsored. One speaker strongly supported the idea, saying she had seen fitness influencers push supplements without disclosing payment, which felt dishonest and even risky for consumers who trust them. The next speaker was more hesitant, arguing that such rules would be difficult to enforce, since influencers could use vague hashtags that technically comply without really informing anyone, and that not every product mention is actually paid. To resolve this, the first speaker suggested the rule apply only to genuine paid partnerships or gifted products, and proposed that platforms build an automatic disclosure tool directly into the app, removing the guesswork from wording. The others agreed this was a better solution, since it shifts responsibility to the platform rather than relying on influencers to word things honestly themselves, and it also protects honest creators from being unfairly grouped with dishonest ones. In the end, they agreed to recommend mandatory platform-level tagging for paid content instead of trusting individual disclosure.',
