  42 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: I read an article suggesting that parents should limit children\'s screen time to no more than two hours a day, including tablets, phones, and television combined. It got me thinking about whether such a strict rule is actually realistic in today\'s world.
Speaker 2: I actually think that number is reasonable, maybe even generous. There\'s growing research linking excessive screen exposure in kids to poor sleep, shorter attention spans, and reduced physical activity. Setting a firm limit teaches children healthy habits early, before screens become an uncontrollable habit.
Speaker 3: I understand the health concerns, but two hours feels unrealistic given how much schoolwork now happens digitally. My nephew does homework on a tablet, reads assigned e-books, and even has group projects that require video calls. Counting all of that against a strict two-hour cap seems impractical and could unfairly limit his education.
Speaker 1: That\'s a good point, maybe the guideline needs to distinguish between passive entertainment, like watching videos or playing games, and active or educational screen use, like schoolwork or learning apps. A limit focused specifically on recreational screen time would address the health concerns without penalizing education.
Speaker 2: I can accept that distinction. The real worry was always about mindless scrolling and endless entertainment apps eating into sleep and outdoor play, not about a child researching a school project or joining a class discussion online.
Speaker 3: That resolves my concern too. If we separate educational use from recreational use, and only cap the recreational portion at around two hours, it protects both his schoolwork and his overall wellbeing. I think that\'s the fair compromise the whole group can support.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group discussed whether children\'s screen time should be strictly limited to two hours a day, as suggested in an article one speaker had read. One speaker agreed with the limit, citing research connecting excessive screen use in children to poor sleep, shorter attention spans, and less physical activity, and felt a firm cap builds healthy habits early. The other speaker pushed back, explaining that a lot of modern schoolwork happens on screens, giving the example of a nephew doing homework, reading e-books, and joining video calls for group projects, so a strict two-hour cap would unfairly restrict his education. To resolve this, the first speaker suggested distinguishing between passive, recreational screen use like videos and games, versus active, educational use like schoolwork, and applying the two-hour limit only to the recreational side. The second speaker accepted this, clarifying that the real concern was always mindless entertainment cutting into sleep and outdoor play, not learning. The third speaker agreed too, and the group concluded that separating educational from recreational screen time, while capping only the latter, was the fairest solution for everyone.',
