  43 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: With so many people cancelling cable subscriptions in favor of streaming services, I wanted to get your thoughts on whether traditional television still has a place at all, or if it\'s basically becoming obsolete within the next decade.
Speaker 2: Personally, I think cable is on its way out for good reason. Streaming gives you control, you watch what you want, whenever you want, without sitting through the same commercial breaks every ten minutes. Plus, you\'re not locked into paying for hundreds of channels you never actually watch.
Speaker 3: I see the appeal, but I still think live television has a unique advantage that streaming hasn\'t fully replaced, especially for things like live sports, breaking news, or major events. There\'s something about watching a game with the whole country at the same moment that a delayed or on-demand stream just can\'t replicate.
Speaker 1: That\'s a fair distinction. Maybe it\'s less about cable disappearing entirely and more about it shrinking down to serve those specific live-event needs, while everything else, like scripted shows and movies, moves permanently to streaming platforms where people already prefer to watch them.
Speaker 2: That actually makes sense to me. I\'d still cancel my full cable package, but I could see paying for a smaller live-sports or news streaming add-on instead of the entire bundle. It\'s really the bundling of channels nobody watches that bothers me, not live content itself.
Speaker 3: Right, and that\'s already happening with some sports streaming apps. So I think we can agree that full cable bundles are dying out, but a leaner, live-focused streaming option will likely survive alongside the on-demand platforms for regular entertainment.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group discussed whether traditional cable television still has a future given the rise of streaming services. One speaker argued that cable is essentially dying because streaming offers control over what and when to watch, without repetitive commercials or paying for unwanted channels. The other speaker disagreed somewhat, pointing out that live television still has an edge for sports, breaking news, and major events, since watching something happen live with everyone else is an experience streaming hasn\'t fully replicated. The first speaker then suggested a middle ground, proposing that cable won\'t disappear completely but will shrink to serve only those live-event needs, while scripted shows and movies move fully to streaming. The second speaker agreed, adding that they\'d still cancel a full cable package but would consider paying for a smaller live-sports or news streaming add-on instead of a full bundle, since the real issue was paying for unused channels, not live content itself. The third speaker confirmed this is already happening with some apps. In the end, the group agreed that full cable bundles are declining, but leaner, live-focused streaming options will likely survive.',
