  49 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: I noticed our university library is planning to cut its physical book budget significantly and shift almost entirely toward e-books and digital subscriptions. I wanted to get your thoughts on whether that\'s actually a good direction for them to take.
Speaker 2: I think it makes a lot of sense, honestly. E-books are searchable, don\'t take up physical shelf space, and multiple students can access the same digital textbook simultaneously instead of waiting for someone to return a single physical copy. It\'s just more efficient for a busy campus library.
Speaker 3: I see the convenience, but I still read and retain information much better from a physical book. Research also suggests many students get more easily distracted or experience more eye strain reading long texts on a screen compared to paper. Removing physical books almost entirely feels like it could hurt learning outcomes for some students.
Speaker 1: That\'s an important point, efficiency shouldn\'t come at the expense of actual comprehension and learning. Maybe the library shouldn\'t eliminate physical books completely, but instead keep a smaller, curated physical collection of core textbooks and popular titles, while moving the bulk of niche or rarely used materials to digital formats.
Speaker 2: That seems reasonable to me. Honestly, most of the space savings come from removing books that only get checked out once or twice a year anyway, so keeping the frequently used core texts in print wouldn\'t undo much of the efficiency benefit.
Speaker 3: I\'d be satisfied with that compromise, since students who prefer paper would still have access to the essential texts, while everything else moves digital for convenience. I think we should recommend a hybrid collection rather than a full digital switch.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group discussed a university library\'s plan to cut its physical book budget and move almost entirely to e-books. One speaker supported the shift, arguing e-books are searchable, save shelf space, and allow multiple students to access the same textbook at once instead of waiting for a physical copy. The other speaker disagreed, saying they personally retain information better from physical books, and cited research suggesting screens cause more distraction and eye strain, which could hurt some students\' learning outcomes. The first speaker then proposed a middle ground, suggesting the library keep a smaller, curated collection of core textbooks and popular titles in print, while moving rarely used or niche materials to digital formats. The second speaker agreed, pointing out that most space savings come from rarely checked-out books anyway, so keeping core texts in print wouldn\'t undo much benefit. The third speaker was satisfied with this compromise since it preserves access for students who prefer paper. In the end, the group agreed to recommend a hybrid collection instead of a complete switch to digital.',
