  76 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: The transit authority has funding for one major project connecting the suburbs to downtown, and they\'re deciding between extending the light rail line or building a bus rapid transit corridor with dedicated lanes. They\'ve asked our advisory group to weigh in before finalizing the plan.
Speaker 2: I favor the light rail extension. Trains have much higher capacity than buses, and rail tends to attract more long-term investment and development along its route since it\'s a permanent fixture, unlike bus routes that can just be changed or cancelled later.
Speaker 3: The problem is cost and time. Light rail construction is enormously expensive and could take a decade to finish, while bus rapid transit with dedicated lanes could be running within two years at a fraction of the cost, getting people out of cars much sooner rather than waiting a decade for benefits.
Speaker 1: What if we recommend starting with the bus rapid transit corridor now to get quick relief for commuters, while simultaneously reserving the corridor\'s right-of-way and design so it could be converted or upgraded into light rail later once ridership numbers justify the bigger investment?
Speaker 2: I could accept that as a reasonable staged approach, since it doesn\'t rule out rail entirely, and reserving the corridor now protects the option for later instead of losing it to other development.
Speaker 3: That works well for me too, since we get the fast, affordable relief from buses now without closing the door on rail down the road. I\'ll recommend the phased approach starting with bus rapid transit.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The regional transit advisory group discussed whether to spend available funding on extending the light rail line or building a bus rapid transit corridor to connect the suburbs to downtown. The first speaker introduced the choice facing the transit authority. One person favored light rail, citing its higher capacity and tendency to attract long-term development along permanent routes. The other pointed out those rail projects are extremely expensive and could take a decade to complete, while bus rapid transit could be running within two years at a fraction of the cost. The first speaker proposed starting with bus rapid transit now for quick relief, while reserving the corridor\'s design and right-of-way so it could later be upgraded to light rail once ridership justified it. The second speaker accepted this staged approach since it didn\'t rule out rail permanently. The third speaker also agreed, and the group recommended the phased plan, starting with bus rapid transit and preserving the option to upgrade to light rail later.',
    'voices' => [
      'af_aoede',
      'am_fenrir',
      'af_heart',
    ],
  ],
  77 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: The state is considering a bottle deposit scheme where consumers pay a small extra deposit on plastic bottles at purchase, refunded when they return the empty bottle to a collection point. Our panel has been asked to weigh in before it goes to the legislature.
Speaker 2: I think it\'s a proven idea. States and countries with deposit schemes consistently see recycling rates for bottles above ninety percent, way higher than curbside recycling alone achieves, since the direct cash incentive actually gets people to return bottles instead of tossing them in general trash.
Speaker 3: My worry is the burden on small retailers who\'d have to install and maintain reverse vending machines or manually handle returns, which takes staff time and store space they may not have, especially small convenience stores that already operate on thin margins. Storing returned bottles until pickup also takes up valuable shelf and storage space that small shops simply don\'t have to spare.
Speaker 1: What if the law required deposit collection points at any store above a certain size, but allowed smaller convenience stores to opt out if a collection point exists within a short walking distance, say five hundred meters, run by a nearby larger retailer instead?
Speaker 2: That seems sensible, since it still guarantees convenient access to returns for most consumers while not forcing every tiny shop to install expensive machinery it can\'t really afford.
Speaker 3: I\'m fine with that version, since it protects small retailers without weakening the overall return network for consumers. I\'ll tell the panel we support the deposit scheme with a size-based exemption for small stores near existing collection points.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The state environmental panel discussed a proposed bottle deposit scheme, where consumers pay a small deposit on plastic bottles refunded upon return. The first speaker introduced the idea ahead of a legislative vote. One person supported it strongly, citing other regions where deposit schemes pushed bottle recycling rates above ninety percent, far higher than curbside recycling alone. The other worried about the burden on small retailers who would need to install and maintain return machines or handle returns manually, which many can\'t afford. The first speaker proposed requiring collection points only at stores above a certain size, letting smaller convenience stores opt out if a collection point exists within five hundred meters at a larger nearby retailer. The second speaker agreed this maintained convenient access without forcing tiny shops into expensive installations. The third speaker accepted the compromise, and the panel\'s recommendation was to support the deposit scheme with a size-based exemption for small stores near existing collection points.',
    'voices' => [
      'bm_lewis',
      'bm_george',
      'am_echo',
    ],
  ],
  78 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: The building code committee is proposing that all newly constructed homes include a rainwater harvesting system, collecting runoff from the roof into a tank for use in gardening and toilet flushing. They want feedback before adding it as a mandatory requirement in the updated code.
Speaker 2: I support making it mandatory. With water shortages becoming more frequent, every new home built without this system is a missed opportunity, and it\'s much cheaper to install a tank during construction than retrofitting one later, so requiring it upfront just makes long-term sense for the whole region.
Speaker 3: I think mandating it adds a meaningful cost to new home construction at a time when housing affordability is already a huge problem. Builders will likely just pass that extra cost straight onto buyers, making already expensive new homes even less attainable for first-time buyers trying to enter the market.
Speaker 1: What if the code required the plumbing to be pre-installed for a future rainwater tank, meaning the pipes and connection points are built in, but made the tank itself optional, with a rebate available for homeowners who choose to add it either at construction or later?
