Speaker 3: That eases my affordability concern significantly, since the core cost increase is much smaller. I\'ll recommend requiring pre-installed plumbing with an optional tank and rebate, rather than a fully mandatory system.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The building code committee discussed whether to require rainwater harvesting systems in all newly constructed homes, collecting roof runoff for gardening and toilet use. The first speaker introduced the proposal ahead of a code update. One person supported making it mandatory, arguing installation during construction is far cheaper than retrofitting later and water shortages are becoming more frequent. The other worried that mandating the full system would raise construction costs, which builders would pass on to buyers, worsening housing affordability. The first speaker proposed requiring only the plumbing and connection points to be pre-installed, making the actual tank optional with a rebate available for homeowners who add it later. The second speaker agreed this solved the expensive-retrofit problem while keeping upfront costs low. The third speaker accepted this since it eased the affordability concern significantly, and the committee recommended requiring pre-installed plumbing with an optional tank and rebate rather than a fully mandatory system.',
