  82 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: I saw that our HR department is debating whether to officially allow mental health days as a separate category of leave, rather than making employees use regular sick days or vacation time for them. What do you two think about that?
Speaker 2: I think it\'s long overdue. Mental health is just as real as physical health, and forcing people to either lie about being physically sick or burn through their vacation days when they\'re mentally exhausted sends the message that emotional wellbeing doesn\'t matter as much. Having dedicated mental health days would reduce stigma and encourage people to actually rest before they burn out completely.
Speaker 3: I understand the intention, but I worry it could be difficult to manage fairly in practice. How do you verify a mental health day versus someone just wanting an extra day off? It could create resentment among coworkers if some people take frequent mental health days and others end up covering their workload without any real oversight.
Speaker 1: That\'s a valid concern about fairness, but maybe the solution isn\'t to avoid the policy altogether, but rather to set a reasonable limit, like a few dedicated mental health days per year, similar to how sick leave already has a cap. That way people get support without it becoming unlimited or open to abuse.
Speaker 2: I like that compromise. It also normalizes the idea without requiring people to explain themselves in detail, since privacy really matters here, nobody should have to justify their mental state to a manager just to take a day off.
Speaker 3: Fair enough, a capped allowance sounds much more manageable than an open-ended policy. So I think our suggestion to HR should be introducing a set number of mental health days annually, treated with the same privacy as sick leave, rather than either ignoring the issue or leaving it unlimited.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group discussed a proposal from HR to introduce official mental health days as a separate category of leave, rather than making employees use sick days or vacation time. One speaker strongly supported the idea, arguing that mental health deserves the same recognition as physical health and that dedicated days off would reduce stigma and prevent burnout. The other speaker raised concerns about fairness and management, worrying that it would be hard to verify genuine need and could cause resentment if colleagues had to cover for others without any oversight. The first speaker then proposed a compromise, capping mental health days at a set number per year, similar to how sick leave is already limited, so the benefit exists without being open to abuse. The second speaker agreed and added that this approach also protects privacy, since employees shouldn\'t have to explain their mental state to a manager. The third speaker accepted this solution. In the end, the group agreed to recommend a set annual allowance of mental health days, kept as private as sick leave, rather than leaving the policy unlimited or not implementing it at all.',
