  83 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: Have you seen this challenge going around where people are doing a thirty-day social media detox, completely deleting apps like Instagram and TikTok from their phones? A few people in our building are apparently trying it out this month.
Speaker 2: I actually think it\'s a fantastic idea. Constant scrolling has been linked to anxiety, poor sleep, and that endless comparison trap where you measure your life against everyone\'s highlight reel online. Taking a full break lets your brain reset and you often realize how much time you were wasting without even noticing.
Speaker 3: I get the appeal, but a complete detox seems extreme and honestly unrealistic for a lot of people. Social media is also how many of us keep in touch with friends who live far away, find community groups, or even get quick news updates. Cutting it off entirely could leave people feeling more isolated rather than less.
Speaker 1: That\'s true, it does serve real purposes beyond just mindless scrolling. Maybe instead of a total detox, a more sustainable approach would be setting specific time limits, like using an app timer to cap usage at thirty minutes a day, so you keep the benefits while cutting the excess.
Speaker 2: That seems like a smarter long-term strategy than an all-or-nothing detox, since most people end up reinstalling the apps within a week anyway once the initial challenge ends, and then they\'re right back to old habits without having built any lasting change.
Speaker 3: I agree, gradual limits feel more sustainable than quitting cold turkey. So overall, we think a full detox might work as a short-term reset, but building daily time limits is a more realistic long-term solution for maintaining a healthier relationship with social media.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group talked about a trend of people doing thirty-day social media detoxes by deleting apps like Instagram and TikTok completely. One speaker supported the idea, explaining that constant scrolling is linked to anxiety, poor sleep and unhealthy comparison, and that a full break helps reset the brain. The other speaker was more skeptical, arguing that a complete detox is extreme and unrealistic, since social media also helps people stay connected with distant friends, find communities, and get quick news updates, so cutting it off entirely could increase isolation. The first speaker acknowledged this and suggested a middle ground of setting daily time limits using app timers instead of quitting entirely, allowing people to keep the benefits while reducing excessive use. The second speaker agreed this was more sustainable, noting that many people who do a full detox just reinstall the apps afterwards and return to old habits. The third speaker agreed that gradual limits feel more realistic than quitting cold turkey. In the end, the group concluded that while a short detox might work as an initial reset, setting daily time limits is a more practical long-term solution for a healthier relationship with social media.',
