  85 => [
    'transcript' => 'Speaker 1: I noticed almost everyone at the gym is wearing some kind of sleep tracker now, whether it\'s a smartwatch or a ring, that tells them exactly how many hours of deep sleep versus light sleep they got each night. Do you think these devices are actually helping people sleep better?
Speaker 2: I think they can be really useful. Before I started tracking my sleep, I had no idea how inconsistent my schedule was, going to bed at wildly different times every night. Seeing the data laid out made me realize I needed a more consistent routine, and my sleep quality has genuinely improved since then.
Speaker 3: I\'ve actually read about a condition called orthosomnia, where people become so obsessed with getting a perfect sleep score that they end up more anxious and sleep worse as a result. Ironically, checking the app first thing every morning and stressing over a low score can create the very insomnia the device was supposed to help prevent.
Speaker 1: That\'s an interesting paradox, the tool meant to reduce sleep problems ends up causing them for certain personality types. It seems like the issue isn\'t the technology itself, but how obsessively people engage with it. Maybe the advice should be to use trackers for general pattern awareness rather than checking scores daily.
Speaker 2: That makes sense, I suppose using it more like a monthly check-in to spot broad trends, rather than an obsessive daily report card, would give the benefits without the anxiety spiral. It\'s really about moderation in how you interact with the data.
Speaker 3: Exactly, and if someone notices they\'re getting more stressed about their sleep score than actually sleeping, that\'s probably a clear sign to put the device away for a while. So our conclusion is that sleep trackers can help, but only when used mindfully rather than obsessively.',
    'sample_answer' => 'The group discussed whether sleep-tracking wearables, now common among gym-goers, actually help people sleep better. One speaker was positive, explaining that seeing her sleep data revealed how inconsistent her schedule was and helped her build a more regular routine that improved her sleep quality. The other speaker raised a concern about a condition called orthosomnia, where people become so fixated on achieving a perfect sleep score that the resulting anxiety actually worsens their sleep, creating the very problem the device was meant to solve. The first speaker found this an interesting paradox and suggested the issue lies in how obsessively people use the technology rather than the technology itself, proposing that trackers be used to spot general patterns rather than checked daily. The second speaker agreed, comparing it to a monthly check-in rather than a daily report card, which would preserve the benefits while avoiding anxiety. The third speaker added that if someone notices more stress about their score than actual sleep, that\'s a sign to stop using the device for a while. The group concluded that sleep trackers can be helpful, but only when used mindfully rather than obsessively.',
    'voices' => [
